---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="National Minimum Wage 2026/27 UK: Rates, Rights & Take-Home Pay | wagecheck"
  description="Complete guide to the UK National Minimum Wage and National Living Wage for 2026/27. Current rates by age, apprentice rates, your rights, and take-home pay."
  breadcrumbName="Minimum Wage Guide"
>
  <section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-50 via-white to-lime-50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 lg:py-20">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.24em] text-emerald-700">Guide</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">
        National Minimum Wage 2026/27 UK: <span class="font-display italic text-emerald-600">Rates, Rights & Take-Home Pay</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-600">
        Everything you need to know about the UK minimum wage for 2026/27 — including hourly rates by age, apprentice pay, your legal rights, and how much you actually take home after tax.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-500">Last updated: September 2026 · Tax year 2026/27</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none space-y-8 text-base leading-7 text-slate-700">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Current minimum wage rates (2026/27)</h2>
        <p>
          From April 2026 the National Minimum Wage and National Living Wage rates are as follows. These are the legal minimum hourly rates that most workers in the UK are entitled to receive.
        </p>

        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 text-left text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500">
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Age Group</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Rate</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3">Annual (37.5 hrs)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">21 and over (NLW)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-emerald-700">£12.71/hour</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">£24,761</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">18–20</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-emerald-700">£10.00/hour</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">£19,500</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-slate-100">
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Under 18</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-emerald-700">£7.55/hour</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">£14,723</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-slate-900">Apprentice (under 19 or first year)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-emerald-700">£7.55/hour</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">£14,723</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          The National Living Wage (NLW) is the rate for workers aged 21 and over. It was previously 23+, but the age threshold was lowered to 21 from April 2024. The annual figure assumes a standard 37.5-hour working week over 52 weeks.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">National Minimum Wage vs Living Wage</h2>
        <p>
          The terms are often confused but they mean different things. The <strong>National Living Wage</strong> is the legal minimum for workers aged 21+. The <strong>Real Living Wage</strong> is a voluntary rate calculated by the Living Wage Foundation based on the actual cost of living. As of 2026 the Real Living Wage is approximately £13.15/hour outside London and £14.40/hour in London, but not all employers pay it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The legal minimum you must be paid is the National Minimum Wage or National Living Wage — whichever applies to your age. If your employer pays below this, you have the right to complain to HMRC and claim back pay.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Who is entitled?</h2>
        <p>
          Almost all workers in the UK are entitled to the minimum wage, including:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Full-time and part-time employees</li>
          <li>Agency workers</li>
          <li>Zero-hours contract workers</li>
          <li>Apprentices (at the apprentice rate during their first year or if under 19)</li>
          <li>Workers on probation (once their employment has started)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Self-employed people, company directors, volunteers and workers on government training schemes are not entitled to the minimum wage. There are also exemptions for some live-in workers and au pairs.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Apprentice rates explained</h2>
        <p>
          Apprentices under 19, or aged 19+ but in their first year of an apprenticeship, are entitled to the apprentice rate of £7.55/hour. Once an apprentice turns 19 and has completed their first year, they move to the age-appropriate rate (18–20 or NLW).
        </p>
        <p>
          Apprentices must be employed for at least 30 hours per week and are entitled to the same rights as other workers, including holiday pay and rest breaks. The apprenticeship must be a genuine programme of learning leading to a recognised qualification.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">What if my employer pays less?</h2>
        <p>
          If your employer is paying below the minimum wage, you should first raise it with them directly. If that doesn't resolve the issue, you can contact the HMRC Minimum Wage helpline on 0800 917 2668. HMRC can investigate, order your employer to pay the arrears, and impose financial penalties.
        </p>
        <p>
          Employers who fail to pay the minimum wage can be fined up to 200% of the arrears, capped at £20,000 per worker. They may also be publicly named by the government. It is illegal for an employer to dismiss or treat you unfairly for enquiring about or complaining about the minimum wage.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">How much do minimum wage workers take home?</h2>
        <p>
          At the National Living Wage of £12.71/hour for 37.5 hours per week, your annual gross salary is £24,761. After income tax, National Insurance and a 5% pension contribution, you would take home approximately £21,200 per year (£1,767 per month).
        </p>
        <p>
          At the 18–20 rate of £10.00/hour, the annual gross is £19,500. After tax, NI and pension, the take-home is approximately £17,000 per year (£1,417 per month). Note that workers on the 18–20 rate pay less income tax and NI because their income is closer to the Personal Allowance.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-2xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 p-6">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Minimum Wage Calculator</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">
          Use our <a href="/national-minimum-wage/" class="font-semibold text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700">minimum wage calculator</a> to see your hourly rate, monthly pay and take-home after tax for 2026/27. You can also try our <a href="/hourly-wage-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-700">hourly wage calculator</a> to convert any rate to an annual salary.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
